### What is a shadow deployment?

A shadow deployment, also called traffic mirroring or dark launch, runs a new version of a service alongside the current one. The live version serves every real response. The shadow version silently processes the mirrored requests for comparison.

```text
Shadow deployment flow:

  Client Request
      │
      ▼
  ┌────────────────────────┐
  │  Routing Layer          │
  │  (Gateway / Mesh)       │
  └───┬────────────┬────────┘
      │            │ (copy)
      ▼            ▼
  ┌────────┐  ┌────────────┐
  │  LIVE  │  │  SHADOW    │
  │  v1.0  │  │  v2.0      │
  │        │  │            │
  │Response│  │Response    │
  │returned│  │DISCARDED   │
  └────────┘  └────────────┘
```

### Why is side-effect isolation critical?

If the shadow service shares the live database and writes to it, mirrored traffic will double-write records. If it calls the real payment rail, it will move real money twice.

```java
package com.bank.shadow;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class ShadowServiceConfig {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(ShadowServiceConfig.class.getName());

    // WRONG: Shadow shares live database and payment rail.
    public void wrongShadow(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // liveDatabase.save(payment);  <- double-writes records
        // paymentRail.send(amount);    <- moves real money twice
        LOG.severe("Shadow caused real side effects.");
    }

    // CORRECT: Shadow uses isolated datastore and mocked externals.
    public void correctShadow(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // shadowDatabase.save(payment);  <- isolated, no real impact
        // noopPaymentRail.send(amount);  <- no-op, money never moves
        LOG.info("Shadow processed safely in isolation.");
    }
}
```

```text
Isolation requirements for banking:
- Separate database or read-only connection
- Payment rail: no-op or sandbox mode
- Notifications: disabled (no SMS, no email)
- Kafka: separate topic or no-op producer
- Compliance: same encryption and access controls as production
```

### How do you implement response comparison?

You capture the live and shadow responses for the same request and compare them, flagging mismatches in status, body, or timing.

```java
package com.bank.shadow;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class ResponseComparator {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(ResponseComparator.class.getName());

    public void compare(String requestId, String liveResponse, String shadowResponse,
                        long liveLatencyMs, long shadowLatencyMs) {
        if (!liveResponse.equals(shadowResponse)) {
            LOG.warning("MISMATCH requestId=" + requestId
                + " live=" + liveResponse
                + " shadow=" + shadowResponse);
        }
        if (shadowLatencyMs > liveLatencyMs * 2) {
            LOG.warning("SLOW SHADOW requestId=" + requestId
                + " liveMs=" + liveLatencyMs
                + " shadowMs=" + shadowLatencyMs);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected comparison output:
MATCH:    requestId=R001 live={balance:50000} shadow={balance:50000}
MISMATCH: requestId=R002 live={balance:0} shadow={balance:1.00}
  -> Edge case bug: zero-principal account returns 1 rupee in shadow.
  -> Customer got correct live answer (zero impact).
  -> Bug found before promotion.
```

### How do you implement mirroring in Istio?

In Istio, mirroring is a first-class VirtualService feature. You specify a mirror destination and a mirror percentage.

```yaml
# Istio VirtualService with traffic mirroring
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: interest-calculation
spec:
  hosts:
    - interest-service
  http:
    - route:
        - destination:
            host: interest-service
            subset: stable  # live version
      mirror:
        host: interest-service
        subset: shadow  # new version
      mirrorPercentage:
        value: 10.0  # mirror 10% of traffic
```

```text
Expected behavior:
100% of traffic served by stable (live responses to clients)
10% of traffic copied to shadow (responses discarded)
Shadow environment has isolated DB and no-op externals.
```

### How do you implement mirroring in Spring Cloud Gateway?

In Spring Cloud Gateway, implement a filter that dispatches to the primary route and asynchronously fires a duplicate to the shadow.

```java
package com.bank.gateway.filter;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilter;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilterChain;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Component
public class ShadowMirrorFilter implements GatewayFilter {

    private final WebClient shadowClient;

    public ShadowMirrorFilter(WebClient.Builder builder) {
        this.shadowClient = builder.baseUrl("http://shadow-interest-service").build();
    }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        // Fire-and-forget: never delay the real response
        shadowClient.method(exchange.getRequest().getMethod())
            .uri(exchange.getRequest().getURI().getPath())
            .exchange()
            .subscribe(); // result discarded

        return chain.filter(exchange); // real response proceeds normally
    }
}
```

```text
Key: fire-and-forget. A slow shadow NEVER delays the real response.
```

### How is shadow different from canary? (comparison table)

| Aspect | Shadow | Canary |
|---|---|---|
| Serves real responses | No (discarded) | Yes (to a slice of users) |
| User risk | Zero | Some (small slice) |
| Side effects | Isolated/sandboxed | Real |
| Purpose | Validate correctness before exposure | Gradual rollout with real traffic |
| Order | Shadow first, then canary | After shadow proves safe |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is failing to isolate side effects.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

// WRONG: Shadow shares live database.
public class WrongShadow {
    // Mirrored transfer writes to live payments table -> double records
    // Mirrored notification sends real SMS -> customer confused
}

// CORRECT: Shadow uses isolated DB and no-op externals.
public class CorrectShadow {
    // shadowPaymentsDb -> isolated, periodically wiped
    // noOpNotifier -> logs but does not send
    // noOpPaymentRail -> no money moves
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Mirroring doubles the load on the mirrored path, so mirror only a sampled percentage and ensure fire-and-forget so a slow shadow never delays real responses. Mirrored requests carry real customer data, so the shadow environment must meet the same security and compliance controls as production. Shadow is the safer first step before a canary.
